<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Opportunity radar — local fallback for accounts without cloud routines.
  Runs the same prompt through `claude -p` on a schedule. It only fires while the Mac is awake,
  which is a real downgrade from a cloud routine; say so before installing it.

  Replace USERNAME, the prompt path, and the weekday/hour below.
  StartCalendarInterval fires in LOCAL time, unlike a cloud cron, which is UTC.
  Weekday: 0 or 7 = Sunday, 1 = Monday, … 5 = Friday.

  The bot token is NOT in this file. The prompt reads ~/.radar_bot_token (chmod 600), so the
  prompt's delivery block must say `$(cat ~/.radar_bot_token)` where the cloud version says
  `$SLACK_BOT_TOKEN`. Note what that costs: the local session reads the token into its own context,
  and therefore into its transcript. That breaks the rule the cloud path keeps. Say so before
  installing this, and treat the transcript as sensitive afterwards.

  --allowedTools is what keeps an unattended run from stalling at a permission prompt. It must
  list every tool the prompt uses; `--permission-mode acceptEdits` is NOT enough, because it
  auto-accepts edits only and the radar's delivery step is a Bash curl. Prefer this explicit list
  over --dangerously-skip-permissions.

  A local run uses the LOCAL MCP servers, not the claude.ai connectors the cloud prompt is written
  against. If the prompt reads mail through a cloud connector, that step will not work here; either
  wire an equivalent local server or cut the mail item and say so.

  launchd runs a missed calendar job once at the next wake, so a sleeping Mac produces a digest at
  an unpredictable hour rather than none at all — which matters, because the delivery design is
  built around when the ping arrives. If the Mac is off or the user is logged out, the gui/ domain
  is not loaded and nothing runs.

  Install (the file must be named com.USERNAME.opportunity-radar.plist):
    cp com.USERNAME.opportunity-radar.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.USERNAME.opportunity-radar.plist
    launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.USERNAME.opportunity-radar   # fires a REAL digest now
  Remove (one command, no side effects):
    launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.USERNAME.opportunity-radar && rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.USERNAME.opportunity-radar.plist
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.USERNAME.opportunity-radar</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/zsh</string>
    <string>-lc</string>
    <string>claude -p "$(cat ~/PROJECT/radar/prompt.txt)" --allowedTools Bash Read Write Edit Glob Grep WebSearch WebFetch</string>
  </array>

  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <dict>
    <key>Weekday</key><integer>5</integer>
    <key>Hour</key><integer>9</integer>
    <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/USERNAME/Library/Logs/opportunity-radar.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/USERNAME/Library/Logs/opportunity-radar.log</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <!-- claude is often at ~/.local/bin/claude; check `which claude` and put its dir first. -->
    <string>/Users/USERNAME/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
